# ==============================================================================
# Commit Message Template
# Purpose: Standardize commit messages for clarity, automation, and changelogs.
# Usage: This is pre-filled when using `git commit` (no -m), not enforced.
# ==============================================================================

# --------------------------
# Conventional Commit Format
# --------------------------
# <type>(<scope>)!?: <subject>
#
# > Entire header should be ≤50 characters, in lowercase, and imperative mood.
#
# type: Required. See allowed types below.
# scope: Optional. Affected area (e.g. auth, ui, api).
# !: Optional. Breaking change.
# subject: Required.

# Examples:
# feat(ui): add carousel to landing page
# fix(auth): handle OAuth callback errors
# chore(deps)!: update to latest major versions

# -------------
# Allowed Types
# -------------
# feat:      New feature
# fix:       Bug fix
# docs:      Documentation only changes
# style:     Non-functional formatting (whitespace, commas)
# refactor:  Code change without behavior change
# perf:      Performance improvement
# test:      Adding or improving tests
# build:     Changes that affect the build system
# ci:        Changes to CI config
# chore:     Maintenance tasks
# revert:    Reverts a previous commit

# ------------------
# Body (recommended)
# ------------------
# - Explain *what* and *why*, not *how*
# - Use bullet points for multiple improvements
# - Wrap at 72 characters

# -----------------
# Footer (optional)
# -----------------
# - For breaking changes:
# BREAKING CHANGE: <description>
#
# - For linked issues:
# Closes: #<issue number>, #<issue number>
#
# - Co-authors:
# Co-authored-by: <Name> <email@example.com>

# -----------------------------------------
# Feedback-Driven Template (Optional block)
# -----------------------------------------
# <type>(<scope>)!?: <action per user feedback on specific issue>
#
# User feedback: "<exact quote of user feedback>"
#
# Original issue: <description of what was inadequate/wrong>
#
# Key changes:
# - <specific improvement 1>
# - <specific improvement 2>
# - <specific improvement 3>
#
# <Context-specific section>:
# <relevant details, impacts, or technical notes>
#
# <Summary of outcome and value provided>

# ==============================================================================
# Tip: Git automatically ignores lines starting with `#`.
# Only non-commented lines will be included in the commit message.
# ==============================================================================
